= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <azimuth> measured from north toward west, and positive <hour angle> westward. These conventions are forced by the signs in the coordinate formulae. In the diagram the observer is at $O$, the local vertical meets the <celestial sphere> at the <zenith> $Z$, and the north <celestial pole> is $P$. The <astronomical horizon> is perpendicular to $OZ$; the <celestial equator> is perpendicular to $OP$. Their intersections with the <celestial meridian> give the north horizon point and the upper equatorial meridian point.

\Image[paper-338-celestial-sphere.png]
{height=650}
{title=The <celestial sphere> with horizon and equatorial coordinates}

The north <celestial pole> has <altitude> $\theta$; thus the <angle> between $OP$ and $OZ$ is $90^\circ-\theta$. Project $X$ along its vertical <great circle> onto the <astronomical horizon> at $H$: the arc $HX$ is the <altitude> $\gamma$, and the westward horizon arc from north to $H$ is the <azimuth> $\eta$. Project $X$ along its hour circle onto the <celestial equator> at $Q$: the arc $QX$ is the <declination> $\delta$, and the westward equatorial arc from the upper <celestial meridian> to $Q$ is the <hour angle> $h$. Equivalently the triangle on the <celestial sphere> $PZX$ has sides $PZ=90^\circ-\theta$, $PX=90^\circ-\delta$ and $ZX=90^\circ-\gamma$. **All five <angles> refer to arcs on their specified reference circles, not arbitrary <angles> in the projected drawing.**

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $\mathbf n,\mathbf w,\mathbf u$ be orthonormal <unit vectors> north, west and upward. A direction of <altitude> $\gamma$ and westward <azimuth> $\eta$ is
$$
\mathbf x=\cos\gamma\cos\eta\,\mathbf n+\cos\gamma\sin\eta\,\mathbf w+\sin\gamma\,\mathbf u.
$$
The north <celestial pole> and the upper equatorial meridian direction are
$$
\mathbf p=\cos\theta\,\mathbf n+\sin\theta\,\mathbf u,\qquad
\mathbf e=-\sin\theta\,\mathbf n+\cos\theta\,\mathbf u.
$$
In the <equatorial coordinate system>, the same <unit vector> is $\mathbf x=\sin\delta\,\mathbf p+\cos\delta(\cos h\,\mathbf e+\sin h\,\mathbf w)$. Taking <dot products> with $\mathbf p,\mathbf w,\mathbf e$ respectively gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\sin\delta&=\sin\gamma\sin\theta+\cos\gamma\cos\theta\cos\eta,\\
\cos\delta\sin h&=\sin\eta\cos\gamma,\\
\cos\delta\cos h&=\sin\gamma\cos\theta-\cos\gamma\sin\theta\cos\eta.
\end{aligned}}
$$
This is an <orthogonal transformation> between two <bases>, so it preserves the length of the direction vector. Recover $h$ from both its <sine> and <cosine> to keep the correct quadrant. At the <zenith>, <azimuth> itself is undefined; the vector equations remain meaningful by continuity. Using the more usual eastward <azimuth> changes the sign of the second equation.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Local <sidereal time> is the <right ascension> on the upper <celestial meridian>. Since <right ascension> increases eastward while <hour angle> increases westward, their difference is
$$
\boxed{h=\mathrm{LST}-\alpha\pmod{24\ \mathrm{hours}}.}
$$
One hour of either coordinate is $15^\circ$. For a fixed stellar direction, $h$ increases at the sidereal rotation rate. Mean <sidereal time> should be used with mean <right ascension>, or apparent <sidereal time> with apparent <right ascension>, for the same reference equinox.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $T_{\rm sid}$ for one sidereal day in minutes and $\Omega_{\rm sid}=2\pi/T_{\rm sid}$. An equatorial <star> at an equatorial site rises along a vertical <great circle>, passes through the <zenith>, and descends on the opposite side. Its <azimuth> changes by $\pi$ at the crossing. The restricted <altitude> travel prevents following it continuously by rotating over the <zenith>.

The maximum <azimuth> rate is $\Omega_{\rm az}=2\pi\omega$ radians per minute, so the shortest half-turn takes $\Delta t=\pi/\Omega_{\rm az}=1/(2\omega)$ minutes. During that lost-track interval the <star> moves through
$$
\boxed{\phi_{\rm full}=\Omega_{\rm sid}\Delta t=\frac{\pi}{\omega T_{\rm sid}}\ \mathrm{radians}=\frac{180^\circ}{\omega T_{\rm sid}}.}
$$
For $T_{\rm sid}\simeq1436.07$ minutes this is approximately $0.12534^\circ/\omega$; using a 24-hour day gives $0.125^\circ/\omega$. If the quoted <zenith blind spot> size means the angular radius of a symmetrically placed crossing gap, it is $\phi_{\rm full}/2$, approximately $0.06267^\circ/\omega$.

This derives the ideal crossing-gap size with instantaneous acceleration and an available <altitude> drive. It does not uniquely define a circular forbidden region for all nearby trajectories. For example, at an equatorial site a <star> of small nonzero <declination> $\delta$ has maximum <azimuth> rate $\Omega_{\rm sid}|\cot\delta|$ at transit: its minimum offset is constrained by $|\tan\delta|\ge\Omega_{\rm sid}/\Omega_{\rm az}$. That rate contour and the half-turn crossing gap are different definitions of a <zenith blind spot>. Actual tracking footprints also depend on acceleration and the reacquisition strategy, as discussed in \a[https://www.astro.uni.torun.pl/~kb/HandbRT32/ChapterII.htm][the telescope designers' tracking discussion].

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <star> of <declination> $\delta=\theta$ crosses the <zenith>. Its direction rotates around the <celestial pole> at $\Omega_{\rm sid}$, but its actual <angular velocity> on the <celestial sphere> is $\Omega_{\rm sid}\cos\theta$. Consequently the small-angle crossing-gap estimate becomes
$$
\boxed{\phi_{\rm full}\simeq\frac{\pi\cos\theta}{\omega T_{\rm sid}},\qquad
\phi_{\rm radius}\simeq\frac{\pi\cos\theta}{2\omega T_{\rm sid}}.}
$$
For clarity, the factor $\cos\theta$ is the radius of the <star>'s daily circle on a unit <celestial sphere>; it does not modify the sidereal <hour angle> rate.

The estimate assumes the required slew is nearly $180^\circ$. A more exact ideal symmetric reacquisition calculation is possible. Let the endpoints have <hour angles> $-H,+H$ and suppose $0<\theta<\pi/2$, $H$ small enough that both endpoints are above the <astronomical horizon>. The horizontal direction components are $n=\sin\theta\cos\theta(1-\cos H)$ and $w=\pm\cos\theta\sin H$. Their shortest <azimuth> separation is
$$
\Delta\eta(H)=2\arctan\!\left(\frac{\cot(H/2)}{\sin\theta}\right).
$$
The minimum ideal gap satisfies $2H\Omega_{\rm az}/\Omega_{\rm sid}=\Delta\eta(H)$; its angular separation is $\phi_{\rm full}=2\arcsin(\cos\theta\sin H)$. Expanding for a fast drive gives the boxed expression. At an equatorial site $\Delta\eta=\pi$ exactly. At a geographic pole the direction with $\delta=\theta$ is stationary, so the crossing argument is inapplicable and the limit is zero. A full near-<zenith> rate-limited footprint still requires specifying the trajectory and drive model.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For thin contacting components, <optical powers> add. At <wavelength> $\lambda$ the total <optical power> is $\Phi(\lambda)=\rho_1[n_1(\lambda)-1]+\rho_2[n_2(\lambda)-1]$. An <achromatic lens> has $\Phi_F=\Phi_C$, hence
$$
\rho_1(n_{1F}-n_{1C})+\rho_2(n_{2F}-n_{2C})=0,
\qquad
\boxed{\frac{\rho_1}{\rho_2}=-\frac{n_{2F}-n_{2C}}{n_{1F}-n_{1C}}.}
$$
For normally dispersive <optical glasses>, both index differences are positive, so the two components have opposite curvature factors and opposite <optical powers>. This uses the thin <Lensmaker's equation>; finite thickness and separated principal planes require a more detailed design. If the denominator vanishes, use the undivided power-balance equation rather than this ratio. Two-wavelength correction alone does not eliminate the residual <chromatic aberration> at every intermediate <wavelength>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At the $d$ line, the <focal lengths> obey $1/f_{id}=(n_{id}-1)\rho_i$. Thus
$$
\frac{f_{2d}}{f_{1d}}=\frac{(n_{1d}-1)\rho_1}{(n_{2d}-1)\rho_2}
=-\frac{(n_{1d}-1)(n_{2F}-n_{2C})}{(n_{2d}-1)(n_{1F}-n_{1C})}.
$$
Rearranging gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{f_{2d}}{f_{1d}}=-\frac{(n_{2F}-n_{2C})/(n_{2d}-1)}{(n_{1F}-n_{1C})/(n_{1d}-1)}=-\frac{V_{1d}}{V_{2d}}.}
$$
The last equality uses the <Abbe number> definition. The minus sign signifies a converging and a diverging component, with the normal <optical glass> convention and nonzero finite <focal lengths>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Set $\Phi_i=1/f_{id}$ and $\Phi=1/f_d$. The <achromatic doublet power balance> gives two linear equations:
$$
\Phi_1+\Phi_2=\Phi,\qquad\frac{\Phi_1}{V_{1d}}+\frac{\Phi_2}{V_{2d}}=0.
$$
For distinct nonzero <Abbe numbers>, solving yields $\Phi_1=\Phi V_{1d}/(V_{1d}-V_{2d})$ and $\Phi_2=-\Phi V_{2d}/(V_{1d}-V_{2d})$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{f_{1d}=f_d\frac{V_{1d}-V_{2d}}{V_{1d}},\qquad
f_{2d}=f_d\frac{V_{2d}-V_{1d}}{V_{2d}}.}
$$
Equal <Abbe numbers> force the total <optical power> to vanish under the achromatic condition; they cannot give a nonzero-power thin <achromatic lens>. Closely matched <Abbe numbers> require large opposing component powers and are generally unattractive because of the resulting sensitivity and other <optical aberrations>.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

$V_d$ is the <Abbe number>, a dimensionless inverse measure of <optical dispersion>. Large $V_d$ means weak dispersion. The <Abbe diagram> below spans a representative visible-light <optical glass> range, roughly $1.45\lesssim n_d\lesssim2.0$ and $20\lesssim V_d\lesssim95$. The shaded families are schematic, not exact catalogue boundaries; modern compositions overlap and include high-index <crown glasses>.

\Image[paper-338-glass-map.png]
{height=520}
{title=An Abbe diagram of representative optical glasses}

For a usual positive objective, choose component 1 as a higher-$V_d$ <crown glass> and component 2 as a lower-$V_d$ <flint glass>. Then $f_{1d}>0$, $f_{2d}<0$, and $|f_{2d}|/f_{1d}=V_{1d}/V_{2d}>1$: **the <crown glass> is converging, the <flint glass> diverging, and their dispersive powers cancel while leaving positive net power**. The <Abbe numbers> alone do not set their absolute <focal lengths>; the specified total <focal length> does. As a representative pair, $V_{1d}=64.17$, $V_{2d}=36.37$ gives $f_{1d}\simeq0.4332f_d$, $f_{2d}\simeq-0.7644f_d$. Representative index and dispersion data are given in \a[https://www.schott.com/de-de/products/optical-glass-p1000267][SCHOTT's optical-glass data].

